 ## Overview
-From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes the visibility of geometric primitives (typically triangles) relative to specific viewing regions or points within a 3D environment [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently culling occluded geometry during rendering by shifting the computational burden to an offline phase [S1, unofficial]. Unlike methods that assume axis-aligned cells or require minimal preprocessing (such as [PLP](link) or [cPLP](link)), this approach handles complex, non-axis-aligned geometry but incurs significant preprocessing costs [S1, unofficial].
+From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes which parts of a 3D scene are visible from specific regions of the camera space, allowing for aggressive culling of occluded geometry during rendering [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard face culling by operating on large geometric clusters or regions rather than individual voxel faces, and it often incorporates occluder fusion to reduce the complexity of the visibility test [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) which relies on local adjacency, this method solves the problem of global occlusion for large datasets, albeit at the cost of significant preprocessing time [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method involves offline computation of visibility relationships between regions of interest and the scene geometry, often employing occluder fusion to reduce the number of visible primitives [S1, unofficial].
+The method involves offline analysis of the scene geometry to build a visibility map or occluder hierarchy.
 
